      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Jerusalem’s first post office opened in 1888, marking the community’s formal organization and becoming a hub for rural mail.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/jerusalem_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Cotton dominated Jerusalem’s early economy, with families cultivating the crop from the 1880s until diversification began around World War II.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/jerusalem_arkansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Jerusalem sits quietly in the Ozark foothills, surrounded by scattered homes and farmland with little commercial development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/jerusalem_arkansas",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Jerusalem experiences a humid subtropical climate, with hot, humid summers and mild winters, typical of central Arkansas.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/jerusalem_arkansas",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Jerusalem’s foothill location stayed sparsely populated until the mid‑1800s, when upland settlers sought new farmland after river valleys filled.",
